all right let's go now to charles  stratford he's in mario poll in  southeastern ukraine and charles is in a  car for us charlie just tell us where  you are and what you're doing and what's  happening there  hi darren yeah well you join us now in a  car we've come to do  have a look around  the city i am i'm going to turn the  camera around now so you can see it i  mean there are still people on the  streets it is obviously  increasingly quiet um and in the last 10  minutes or so there have been another  series of loud blasters we cannot  confirm where  um  but as you see you know there are there  are pedestrians  moving around there is light traffic  we're on our way to one of the city's  exits  um to try and get a steer on  how many people are trying to leave the  city  as we've been reporting mariopol of  great strategic importance  one would think to any kind of invasion  by russian forces  largely because of its huge  port  its naval base and potentially as a as a  hub to be able to connect  areas of the east the eastern front in  donbass with what analysts say could be  a plan by putin to connect that with the  section north of crimea to create what  analysts describe as a corridor as you  can see there's people here  waiting by bus stops very very little  traffic  the shelling here started in the early  morning it has been very sporadic  and we are getting reports from  a naval base interestingly  around 100 kilometers west  of  um  west of marriott a town called baradansk  the military there saying that  there were positions in that area that  were being shelled they said  um  they believed from russian forces in  russian annexed crimea  um so yeah i mean as you can see  very very quiet streets um  yeah i'm passing interestingly this is a  bank  so you can see people obviously  desperate to try and um increasingly  desperate to try and get money  um get money out and we have been  expecting some sort of some sort of  cyber attack if you like the network's  obviously still going strong as we  wouldn't be able to bring you these  pictures but it's quite a surreal  experience here as you as you can see on  the streets there are still you know  people walking walking around we've also  heard reports we've been speaking to  people in some of the villages that  we've been visiting in the last  week or so on the ukrainian government  controlled side villages on the front  line  people in those villages saying that  they are hunkered down in their cellars  the shelling has been very heavy  overnight and it was ongoing this  morning and those villages i suppose are  around 50 kilometers from here  we've also had reports as i say which we  cannot confirm  of  a village that we  visited north of kharkiv  um about two weeks ago directly on the  border with russia now this was an area  that had seen absolutely no fighting  whatsoever  in the eight years of the conflict  and certainly according to our sources  uh one source now describing that  village as being having been flattened  um so reports there of heavy shelling  potentially fighting um north of kharkiv  but as i say we are unable to to confirm  that  charlie just just just talk us through  you were you were mentioning earlier  charlie about going down sorry charlie  let me interrupt you there you were  talking earlier about the strategic  importance i mean i think it's worth  just reminding our viewers about the  strategic importance of mario paul you  were saying uh it acts possibly as a  potential hub for some kind of corridor  uh across from donbass uh down to crimea  just just just talk us through how that  would work charlie  well the theory is is that if there was  a push by russian forces and and  separatists russian-backed separatists  from the donbass region  in the east  that push could well be joined by a  symbol a simultaneous push by troops by  russian forces in  crimea they would push up towards towns  like kerason  big cities that we visited in the last  few weeks they would push up north from  that and they would head east  the forces coming out of donbass would  head west and there is a very strategic  road there which i believe is the m14 if  i'm not not  my memory deserves me well the m14 a  very strategic road there to take that  area of of land to control that  and join up russian forces in crimea  with obviously the pro-russia  separatists in the separatist held areas  now the reason why analysts have been  saying this is a potentially important  move  um  and they've been saying this for months  now is because in 2014  the uh  the ukrainians once after after the  russians have taken annexed crimea the  ukrainians took control of the water  resources north of crimea  there are canals there that run off the  dunepa river that supplied up to 85  percent of the peninsula the crimean  peninsula's water now we know that  the russians russia has been building  wells in the subsequent years or trying  to build wells on the crimean peninsula  we know that very quickly  they built a bridge across the kirch  strait to connect  the uh the crimean peninsula with the  russian mainland but the theory is is  that they are still suffering  um  very very difficult conditions with  water so  that  certainly  according to military analysts would be  a viable and not inconceivable option  for russian forces yeah to to create  that corridor from the east the donbass  region um  the separatist control area of the  donbass region to those areas of  northern crimea  charlie um it is extraordinary watching  you driving around in mario pole whilst  this invasion is happening um let me get  a final thought from you charles i mean  there are not many people walking around  on the street  and that's uh  that that could give an indication of  just that  the atmosphere that people are scared is  there an atmosphere of fear of  trepidation uh you know do people think  this is the calm before the storm  well i think what's interesting as well  as you can see the streets here are  considerably busier than the streets we  we saw when we initially came live what  five minutes or so ago there's a lot  more traffic here and the traffic is  moving in both directions and we're  heading in the direction of going out of  town  um certainly the shelling that we heard  earlier we understood was um  was along the coast  um and that's where we were close to  where we we started um started reporting  started talking to you a few minutes ago  um  one thing that we hear those throughout  our travels in this area over the last  few weeks is  it's surprisingly  surprising how how how  seemingly little afraid many people are  in in this uh in this area they've kind  of resigned to the fact that this  conflict has gone on for for eight years  um  and  it's also important to recognize that a  lot of people in this area are very  sympathetic to russia as well  they have a a cultural identity a  language that they use that  is russian so they're they a lot of them  do identify with russia  but the overlying feeling certainly in a  town like mario as you can see this  morning you know this is you can see  traffic moving around there's a there's  a couple there  very relaxed walking around um there's  this feeling that um well surely this  couldn't happen there couldn't be a  battle to take control of this huge city  on the on the as of sea  um  so yeah by and large people are have  have been very sort of controlled in  their in their descriptions of the of  certainly of their emotions there is no  underlying sense of panic that we are  seeing at the moment i mean this is a  this is quite a popular shopping area  um it doesn't look like these people are  rushing to get out of the city does it  so yeah  it's going to be interesting we'll know  more the the closer we get to the outer  the outskirts of of the city but uh  yeah this is where we are at at the  moment i think as i mentioned earlier  it's also important to recognize that  mariopol has a lot of fighters in it a  lot of military and a lot of  paramilitaries in it groups that uh  there's another queue there i think for  a bank yes you can see people queuing up  there  at an atm  yeah the city's got a lot of weapons in  it a lot of our men a lot of men that  are prepared to fight  and  some of these paramilitary groups have  been very successful in defending the  city in in 2014 for example so  if there was to be some sort of push  into the city i think it would be not  inconceivable that things could  deteriorate very rapidly indeed and you  could well be seeing  yes  potentially a lot of civilian casualties

